'''Fisher & Paykel Appliances Holdings Limited''' ({{IPAc-en|ˈ|p|aɪ|k|əl}}) is a [[major appliance]] marketer and designer. The company was founded in 1934 and is based in Auckland, New Zealand.

Originally an importer of domestic refrigerators,<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |date=2013-11-12 |title=1934, Woolf Fisher and Maurice Paykel |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/1934-woolf-fisher-and-maurice-paykel/BG45HTCWHLWMJGW6G5PTULMHXQ/ |access-date=2024-09-09 |website=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |language=en-NZ}}</ref> Fisher & Paykel now holds over 420 [[patents]] and bases its identity on innovative design, particularly in the areas of usability and environmental awareness.<ref name="better">{{cite web |title=Fisher and Paykel |url=http://www.betterbydesign.org.nz/casestudies/homegrown/fisherandpaykel/ |url-status=dead |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20071005023054/http://www.betterbydesign.org.nz/casestudies/homegrown/fisherandpaykel/ |archivedate=5 October 2007 |access-date= |publisher=Better by Design NZ}}</ref>

The company's trademarked appliances include Active Smart refrigerators, AeroTech ovens, DishDrawer dishwashers, Smart Drive washing machines and Smartload top loading dryers. The company also manufactures gas and electric cooktops.<ref name="NZ">{{cite web|publisher=Fisher & Paykel Appliances|title=New Zealand Consumer site|url=http://www.fisherpaykel.com/nz/}}</ref>

In 2004, Fisher & Paykel Appliances purchased the United States–based cookware manufacturer Dynamic Cooking Systems, and Italian cookware company Elba in 2006.

Fisher & Paykel has grown into a global company operating in 50 countries and manufacturing in Thailand, China, Italy and Mexico.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.fisherpaykel.com/eu/company/about/|title=About Fisher & Paykel Appliances Europe|website=www.fisherpaykel.com|access-date=2019-03-15}}</ref> The company had a manufacturing base in Australia for almost 20 years and nearly 70 years in New Zealand, but stated it can no longer compete with low cost labour countries and had to close them.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://mobile.abc.net.au/news/2008-04-17/fisher-and-paykel-to-shut-aust-operations/2406714|title=Fisher and Paykel to shut Aust operations - ABC News (Australian Broadcasting Corporation)|website=mobile.abc.net.au|date=17 April 2008|access-date=2019-03-15}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.smh.com.au/business/fisher--paykel-closes-nz-fridges-factory-20160405-gnyy8y.html|title=Fisher & Paykel closes NZ fridges factory|date=2016-04-05|website=[[The Sydney Morning Herald]] |access-date=2019-03-15}}</ref>

In 2012, [[Haier]] purchased over 90% of Fisher & Paykel Appliance shares. No changes in manufacturing or board of directors were made.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/SB10001424052970204349404578101793819992784|title=Haier Obtains More Than 90% of Fisher & Paykel Shares|newspaper=The Wall Street Journal|date=6 November 2012|publisher=wsj.com|last1=Howard|first1=Rebecca}}</ref>

== History ==
Fisher & Paykel Appliances Ltd was listed publicly in 2001, following the separation of Fisher & Paykel Industries Ltd into [[Fisher & Paykel Healthcare]] Ltd and Fisher & Paykel Appliances Ltd that same year.{{cn|date=September 2024}}

===Fisher & Paykel Industries===
Fisher & Paykel Industries Ltd was founded in 1934 in [[New Zealand]] by [[Woolf Fisher|Sir Woolf Fisher]] and [[Maurice Paykel]]<!-- Q105485738 -->.{{cn|date=September 2024}} The company publicly listed in 1979 with [[authorised capital]] of [[New Zealand dollar|NZ$]] 40 million.

Initially the company was an [[importer]] of [[Crosley Corporation#Appliances|Crosley appliances]], [[Maytag]] and [[Pilot (appliances)|Pilot]] products; in 1938 the company began manufacturing [[Kelvinator]] washing machines under license. This followed the introduction of [[tariff]]s by the [[First Labour Government of New Zealand]].{{cn|date=September 2024}}

In 1956, manufacturing was moved to a purpose-built factory in [[Mount Wellington, New Zealand|Mount Wellington]], [[Auckland]]. This facility included flexible machinery manufacturing techniques developed in tandem with the [[raw material]] suppliers, enabling Fisher & Paykel Industries to greatly increase production.{{cn|date=September 2024}}

In 1955, Fisher and Paykel acquired Dunedin electric oven manufacturer [[Henry Ely Shacklock|H. E. Shacklock Ltd]], which dominated the New Zealand domestic appliance market through the era of Government protectionism. Subsequently, the Shacklock brand was gradually withdrawn from the Fisher and Paykel product range.{{cn|date=September 2024}}

The company began exporting within [[Australasia]] and [[East Asia]] around 1968. At that time the company also manufactured [[cabinets]], [[sparkplug]]s and [[television]]s.{{cn|date=September 2024}}

During the 1980s, the company became more focused on [[research and development]],{{cn|date=September 2024}} resulting in the development of the ECS [[direct drive mechanism]] washing machine affectionately known as 'Gentle Annie' and highly automated production lines.{{cn|date=September 2024}} In 1989, the company opened its first overseas manufacturing facility in [[Cleveland, Queensland|Cleveland]], Australia.{{cn|date=September 2024}}

The company entered the European market in 1992, and by 1994 was exporting to over 80 countries.{{cn|date=September 2024}}

===Fisher & Paykel Appliances===
On 12 November 2001, Fisher & Paykel Industries split into Fisher & Paykel Appliances Holdings Ltd and [[Fisher & Paykel Healthcare]] Corporation Ltd.{{cn|date=September 2024}}

In October 2004, Fisher & Paykel Appliances acquired Dynamic Cooking Systems Inc, a United States manufacturer and distributor of cooking appliances.  Dynamic Cooking Systems was acquired for [[United States dollar|US$]]33 million ([[New Zealand dollar|NZ$]]49.3 million) in a debt-free state, allowing the company to leverage market presence while maintaining its quality of engineering.{{cn|date=September 2024}}

In June 2006, the Italian cookware business Elba was acquired from [[DeLonghi]] for [[Euro|€]]78 million (NZ$158 million).<ref name="current">{{cite web|publisher=Current.com.au |url=http://www.current.com.au/2007/04/23/article/PEOQKTZLTL.html |title=Fisher & Paykel launches Italian-made cooking appliances |date=23 April 2007 |url-status=dead |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20110706103345/http://www.current.com.au/2007/04/23/article/PEOQKTZLTL.html |archivedate=6 July 2011}}</ref> Elba has been since renamed as Fisher & Paykel Appliances Italy S.p.A.<ref name="2007report">{{cite web |date=2007 |title=Annual Report 2006/07 |url=http://www.fisherpaykel.com/nz/company |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160309031423/https://www.fisherpaykel.com/nz/company/ |archive-date=9 March 2016 |publisher=Fisher & Paykel Appliances Holdings Limited}}</ref>{{irrelevant citation|date=November 2023|reason=unsupported by source.}} and exports to over 54 countries, focusing on the UK market.{{cn|date=September 2024}}

Fisher & Paykel makes a relatively broad range of mid to high end dishwashers. Its notable drawcard to the Fisher & Paykel brand is the DishDrawer dishwasher. Most dishwasher brands don't offer drawer-style dishwashers, so Fisher & Paykel is the leader for this niche design. In 2018, Australian customers gave Fisher & Paykel 4 out of 5 stars for overall customer satisfaction for dishwashers.{{cn|date=September 2024}}

==Current products==
Fisher & Paykel Appliances currently manufactures [[cooking]], [[laundry]] and [[white goods|whiteware]] appliances. The company's [[flagship]] product is the DishDrawer double drawer [[dishwasher]] which it claims washes dishes more efficiently than standard dishwashers or hand washing.{{cn|date=September 2024}}

The current Fisher & Paykel-branded product range includes built-in ovens, electric cooktops, dishwashers, dryers, freezers, ranges, [[Extractor hood|rangehoods]], refrigerators and washing machines.{{cn|date=September 2024}}

A large number of Fisher & Paykel products utilise [[microprocessor]] and [[brushless DC electric motor]] technology from [[production line]] equipment to improve efficiency.<ref name="aboutprod">{{cite web |title=Our Products |url=http://www.fisherpaykel.com/nz/company |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160618202647/https://www.fisherpaykel.com/nz/company.html |archive-date=18 June 2016 |access-date= |publisher=Fisher & Paykel Appliances}}</ref>{{irrelevant citation|date=November 2023|reason=unsupported by source to say that the direct drive motors are inspired/based on/source from production line equipment,}}

The company's trademarked brushless motor-based performance features include Smart Drive washing machines, SmartLoad Dryers and DishDrawer dishwashers. Microprocessor-based control systems form the basis of Active Smart refrigerators and AeroTech ovens.{{cn|date=September 2024}}

